    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you also go into more detail about how pH levels influence this process? Specifically, I'm looking at how an alkaline environment vs an acidic one affects the reaction rate and the types of volatile compounds produced.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The pH level has a significant impact on the Maillard reaction. In an alkaline environment, the deprotonation of amino groups increases the concentration of reactive nucleophilic species, thus accelerating the reaction rate. Moreover, an alkaline pH favors the deamination step, leading to the production of amines, which contribute to the aroma and flavor of cooked meat. Conversely, in an acidic environment, the reaction tends to proceed more slowly due to the protonation of amino groups, which reduces their nucleophilicity. However, when the reaction occurs in an acidic milieu, different volatile compounds are formed compared to alkaline conditions, many of which are sulfur-containing compounds that impart a roasted, meaty flavor. The Maillard reaction's pH-sensitive pathways can tailor the sensory attributes of cooked meats, such as the boldness of a crust versus the tenderness of the interior.",
      "source": "target",
